  <!-- FAQ SECTION SLOT (12 COMPREHENSIVE FAQs) -->
  <Fragment slot="faq">
    <div class="space-y-4 text-xs sm:text-sm">
      
      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800" open>
        <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none">
          <h3 class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
            1. What is a profit margin?
            <span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
          </h3>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
          Profit margin measures the percentage of revenue that remains as profit after deducting the cost of goods sold (COGS). It reflects how efficiently a business converts top-line sales into actual earnings.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none">
          <h3 class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
            2. How do I calculate profit margin?
            <span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
          </h3>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
          Subtract your product cost from the selling price to find your profit, divide that profit by the selling price, and multiply by 100. Formula: <code>Margin (%) = (Profit / Revenue) × 100</code>.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none">
          <h3 class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
            3. What is the formula for gross margin?
            <span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
          </h3>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
          The gross margin formula is: <code>Gross Margin (%) = ((Revenue - COGS) / Revenue) × 100</code>, where COGS represents direct cost of goods sold.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none">
          <h3 class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
            4. What is the difference between margin and markup?
            <span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
          </h3>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
          Margin is profit divided by selling price (e.g. ₹20 / ₹50 = 40% margin). Markup is profit divided by cost (e.g. ₹20 / ₹30 = 66.67% markup). Markup is always a higher percentage than margin for the same transaction.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none">
          <h3 class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
            5. How do I calculate a 20% profit margin?
            <span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
          </h3>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
          To achieve a 20% profit margin, divide your unit cost by 0.80 (which is 1 - 0.20). For example, if an item costs ₹80, the required selling price is ₹80 / 0.80 = ₹100, yielding ₹20 profit (20% of ₹100).
        </p>
      </details>

      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none">
          <h3 class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
            6. How do I calculate a 10% profit margin?
            <span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
          </h3>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
          Divide your cost by 0.90 (1 - 0.10). For example, if your cost is ₹90, the selling price is ₹90 / 0.90 = ₹100, providing ₹10 in profit.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none">
          <h3 class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
            7. How do I calculate a 30% profit margin?
            <span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
          </h3>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
          Divide your cost by 0.70 (1 - 0.30). For example, an item costing ₹70 requires a selling price of ₹70 / 0.70 = ₹100 to generate a 30% margin.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none">
          <h3 class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
            8. Can profit margin be 100%?
            <span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
          </h3>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
          In normal commercial operations, no. A 100% margin formula <code>Cost / (1 - 1.00)</code> leads to division by zero. A 100% margin is only theoretically possible if cost is exactly zero.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none">
          <h3 class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
            9. What is the difference between gross and net profit margin?
            <span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
          </h3>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
          Gross profit margin deducts only direct COGS from revenue. Net profit margin deducts all business expenses, including rent, executive salaries, marketing, logistics, taxes, and interest.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none">
          <h3 class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
            10. What is a good profit margin?
            <span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
          </h3>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
          A good margin depends entirely on your industry and business model. Grocery stores thrive on 2%–5% net margins due to rapid inventory velocity, whereas specialized software or consulting firms often exceed 30%–40% margins.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none">
          <h3 class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
            11. Are profit and profit margin the same thing?
            <span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
          </h3>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
          No. Profit is an absolute currency sum (such as ₹20), while profit margin is a relative efficiency ratio expressed as a percentage of revenue (such as 40%).
        </p>
      </details>

      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none">
          <h3 class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
            12. How do I calculate margin in Excel?
            <span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
          </h3>
        </summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
          To calculate margin percentage in Excel with Cost in cell A2 and Revenue in cell B2, enter the formula <code>=(B2-A2)/B2</code> and format as a percentage. To calculate required selling price from Cost in A2 and Desired Margin (e.g. 0.40) in C2, enter <code>=A2/(1-C2)</code>.
        </p>
      </details>

    </div>
